# The demonstrative, which PRECEDES its noun and agrees with it in gender: din
# before a feminine noun (din is-sena, din il-ġimgħa), dan before a masculine
# one (dan ix-xahar, dan il-jum).
#
# "din il" is listed as a surface of its own because Maltese spells the
# feminine demonstrative and the article as one proclitic run before a noun
# taking il-, and that run is what tells "din il-ġimgħa" (this week) apart
# from the weekday Il-Ġimgħa (Friday), which is the same noun under the same
# article.  Gender does the disambiguating: ġimgħa is feminine and takes din,
# while the masculine weekdays keep their own demonstrative frame intact (dan
# il-Ħadd, dan il-Ħamis).  The week reading of "din il-ġimgħa" is the one CLDR
# states -- it is the week field's relative-type-0, and CLDR leaves the mt
# Friday field untranslated rather than assigning the phrase to the weekday --
# and it is the only reading found in running text: mt.wikipedia.org spells it
# "matul din il-ġimgħa, tibda bit-tridu" (Siġġiewi), "Din il-ġimgħa offriet
# kuntrast interessanti" (Inter) and "Matul din il-ġimgħa, issir fiera
# taż-żwiemel" (Ivrea), all of them a span of days.
#
# Unicode CLDR 47, mt dateFields.json, relative-type-0; mt.wikipedia.org,
# articles Siġġiewi, Inter and Ivrea.
din
dan
din il
